{
  "difficulty": "hard",
  "score": 7,
  "estimated_minutes": 65,
  "factors": {
    "transaction_background": {
      "level": "high",
      "note": "The reviewer must understand the Super Senior/Senior Support loss allocation, Assured’s insurance payments, and why reimbursement at section 4.02(a)(3) would divert principal from the certificateholders in Wells Fargo’s interpleader."
    },
    "domain_knowledge": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "Mortgage-securitization waterfalls, realized-loss write-downs, Subsequent Recoveries, and the limited relevance of ERISA eligibility require learning, although the opinion supplies most of the necessary background."
    },
    "defect_spread": {
      "level": "high",
      "note": "Understanding the reimbursement construction requires connecting section 4.02(a)(3), later principal and loss-reimbursement steps, the aggregate and class-specific definitions, section 12.05, and the Policy’s subrogation provision."
    },
    "court_reasoning": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "The court states its subrogation-only holding plainly, but the reviewer must trace its operation through the interest, principal-recovery, and unpaid-loss steps and distinguish that construction from the separate Policy-beneficiary ruling."
    },
    "issue_distinctness": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "The class-specific definition defects and d05–d07 overlap as different aspects of the same reimbursement-versus-subrogation dispute, while d01 concerns the separate meaning of 'Holder' in the Policy."
    }
  },
  "hardest_defect": "d06 — Understanding the surplusage problem requires tracing how the later waterfall payments and class-specific reimbursement amounts operate under the competing readings of section 4.02(a)(3). The reviewer must connect that structure to section 12.05 rather than treating the dispute as merely a dictionary distinction between reimbursement and subrogation.",
  "reason": "Assign this case to a reviewer comfortable mapping interdependent financial provisions, or allow time to build a simple payment-and-loss diagram. The main difficulty is understanding why an insurer that has paid claims does not thereby obtain immediate repayment ahead of Super Senior principal, and how the court instead locates repayment within the insured holders’ distribution rights. Focused rereading of the relevant waterfall steps, reimbursement definitions, and section 12.05 is necessary; the REMIC tax structure and unrelated servicing provisions need not be mastered. The opinion’s explicit holding makes the task manageable once that structure is understood, and the Policy’s 'Holder' issue is comparatively straightforward."
}
